/*
RMSTEST_36 - does grouped_by_team space TEAMS or space PLAYERS
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 15, second attempt

WHY THIS EXISTS - RMSTEST_30 CANNOT BE READ
RMSTEST_30 was run three times at 4v4 with teams correctly set, and the angular
gaps came back strongly bimodal - clusters of 9-43 degrees separated by two arcs
of 119-150. That looks like reading A. It is not evidence for it, for two
reasons that compound:

  1. RMSTEST_30 specifies NO circle_radius, so the DEFAULT ring applies, and
     RMSTEST_24 measured that default as carrying radial variance (sd 7.11)
     AND about +/-7 degrees of angular jitter. Non-uniform spacing is the
     default ring's own behaviour before grouped_by_team touches anything.
  2. Far worse, it used base_size 8 with 200 tiles and returned only 4, 6 and 4
     patches from 8 lands. Adjacent lands MERGED. Merging two neighbours on a
     45-degree ring leaves an apparent gap near 90, and merging three leaves
     135 - which is precisely the 119-150 that was read as team separation.
     The bimodality is fully explained by merging alone.

Both defects push the result toward reading A, which is the reading Sec.6.1
already models, so this is the shape of error that confirms what you expected.

METHOD
Two changes, each removing one defect:
  - circle_radius 40 0 - an EXPLICIT radius with variance EXPLICITLY 0
    (guide:847 gives variance default 0, and RMSTEST_27 shows the default ring
    is not reachable by any other spelling). A perfect ring, so every angular
    deviation is attributable to grouped_by_team and nothing else.
  - base_size 3 with 40 tiles - small lands. At radius 40% of 200 = 80 tiles the
    ring circumference is about 500 tiles, so 45-degree neighbours sit ~63 tiles
    apart and CANNOT merge, while teammates at the documented 2*base_size = 6
    tiles certainly will.

That turns the question into a PATCH COUNT, which is the cheapest possible
reading and needs no angular arithmetic:

  reading A (teams spaced evenly, members packed at 2*base_size)
      5v1v1v1 -> the five merge into ONE patch, three singles -> 4 PATCHES
      4v4     -> 2 PATCHES
  reading B (players spaced evenly, merely reordered so teammates are adjacent)
      5v1v1v1 -> 8 separate patches at ~45 degrees -> 8 PATCHES
      4v4     -> 8 PATCHES

4 against 8 is not a judgement call. Gaps remain available as corroboration.

THREE CONFIGURATIONS:
  36-A  4v4        control, and the one that should show 2 vs 8
  36-C  5v1v1v1    the discriminator

TEAMS ARE SET OUTSIDE THE SCRIPT, in the lobby or the editor's player settings.
The RMSTEST_30 re-run proved the editor CAN set them - it produced a correct
4v4 as [1,3,5,7] vs [2,4,6,8] - so this is known to be workable. What it must
NOT be is the same configuration three times: 4v4 alone cannot separate the
readings under any instrument.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  Patch count is the reading. Patch count near 8 with even ~45 degree gaps is
  reading B; a small count with the merged blob much larger than the singles is
  reading A.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. 36-C returns 4 patches, one of them roughly five times the area of the other
   three. Reading A, Sec.6.1 stands as written.
2. 36-C returns 8 patches at even gaps. Reading B, and Sec.6.1's ring model is
   wrong in a way that matters for every team map.
3. 36-C returns 8 patches at UNEVEN gaps. Neither reading - the members are
   being pulled together without merging, so 2*base_size is not the spacing, and
   guide:356 is describing something else. Record the gap matrix; it becomes the
   measurement.
4. 36-A must return 2 patches under reading A and 8 under B. If 36-A and 36-C
   disagree about which reading holds, the grouping depends on team SIZE, which
   nothing models and which would be the most interesting outcome available.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH 8 PLAYERS.
  Run 36-A at 4v4 twice.
  Run 36-C at 5v1v1v1 three times - it is the discriminator, give it the runs.
Set the teams before generating, and REGENERATE between saves.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  grouped_by_team

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    number_of_tiles 40
    base_size 3
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
    circle_radius 40 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

/* NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> HEADER - an empty one generates cliffs. */

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
